# GitLab work-item board — scripts/work-items.mjs and the Claude Code SessionStart hook that reads it.
# Copy to `.env.gitlab` and fill in. `pnpm items list` to check.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT IN `.env`. mise.toml carries `[env] _.file = ".env"`, so everything in `.env` is exported
# into every shell opened in this repository and inherited by every process started from one — `pnpm dev`,
# the tests, anything. That is correct for the application's own configuration. This token is not that: no
# application process reads it, it grants write access to the issue tracker, and it is the only credential here
# whose blast radius reaches past this machine. mise does not load this file and nothing but the board
# reader reads it. The reader has no fallback to `.env`, deliberately — a fallback would let the token drift
# back into the exported file with nothing to report it.
#
# The token needs the `api` scope. GitLab has no read-only-plus-comment scope, and this both reads the board
# and writes comments, labels and milestones to it (`milestones` creates a `release-*` milestone and moves
# items into it; Reporter is enough for both), so it is as powerful as whoever issued it. Use a PROJECT access
# token on the project below, not a personal one.
#
# An internal GitLab is often VPN-only. Off the VPN every call fails as `network` and the session-start hook stays silent
# by design (exit code 3) — a harness that breaks a session when the VPN is down is worse than no harness.
# Leave this file absent entirely and the same thing happens, so a clone without it is not broken.
GITLAB_URL=https://gitlab.example.com
GITLAB_TOKEN=
GITLAB_PROJECT_ID=
# Optional; default 8000.
GITLAB_TIMEOUT_MS=

# TWO TOKENS, AND WHY. Everything above runs on GITLAB_TOKEN, which is Reporter: it reads the board and
# writes comments and labels, and GitLab does not let Reporter open a merge request. The tempting fix is to
# raise that token to Developer. Don't. GITLAB_TOKEN is fired by the SessionStart hook on every single
# session and by every list, show, comment, label and screenshots call — it is the credential that runs
# constantly, and therefore the last one that should also be able to ship. Opening a merge request happens
# a handful of times per branch.
#
# So the elevated token is the rare one and stays confined to the one subcommand that needs it:
# `pnpm items mr <branch> <file> [title]` is the only reader of GITLAB_MR_TOKEN. Leave it unset and
# everything else works exactly as before; `mr` refuses and says why.
#
# Note this token does NOT push. Branches reach the remote over whoever's git credentials normally push —
# both are independent of these two. Developer is needed for one API call and nothing else.
#
# Before pasting one in, check what it can do beyond opening merge requests. If `master` is protected and
# this token is not on the "allowed to merge" list, it can open a merge request and not accept it, which is
# the intended shape: review stays a human step.
GITLAB_MR_TOKEN=
